SQLite for OpenVMS
==================

This is SQLite @VERSION@ built natively for OpenVMS x86-64 with VSI clang
(64-bit pointers), from the unmodified SQLite release plus the patches and
the OpenVMS file layer of the vms-sqlite project:

    https://github.com/issinoho/vms-sqlite

Kit version: @KITVERSION@.  SQLite and this port are in the public domain;
see COPYING.  The shell is described in SQLITE3.TXT (its manual page), the
API at https://sqlite.org/c3ref/intro.html and in SQLITE3.H.

What is installed
-----------------
    VMSSQLITE$ROOT:[BIN]SQLITE3.EXE             the sqlite3 shell
    VMSSQLITE$ROOT:[LIB]LIBSQLITE3.OLB          the library (object library)
    VMSSQLITE$ROOT:[LIB]SQLITE3$SHR.EXE         the library (shareable image)
    VMSSQLITE$ROOT:[INCLUDE]SQLITE3.H, SQLITE3EXT.H
    VMSSQLITE$ROOT:[COM]VMSSQLITE$SETUP.COM     the DCL symbol sqlite3
    SYS$STARTUP:VMSSQLITE$STARTUP.COM           the logical names

Setting up
----------
The kit installs VMSSQLITE$STARTUP.COM into SYS$STARTUP and runs it once:
it defines the system logical names VMSSQLITE$ROOT and SQLITE3$SHR (the
shareable image).  To define them at every boot, the system manager adds
this line to SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM:

    $ @SYS$STARTUP:VMSSQLITE$STARTUP.COM

Users get the sqlite3 symbol from LOGIN.COM (or SYLOGIN.COM):

    $ @VMSSQLITE$ROOT:[COM]VMSSQLITE$SETUP

The shell
---------
    $ sqlite3 test.db
    $ sqlite3 "-header" "-csv" test.db "select * from t;"

DCL upper-cases arguments that are not quoted: quote the shell's options
and any SQL.  File names may be OpenVMS specifications (DISK:[DIR]TEST.DB)
or Unix-style names (dir/test.db), as from PHP.

Building a program against SQLite
---------------------------------
The library is built with clang, 64-bit pointers.  With clang:

    $ clang -I/vmssqlite$root/include -c myprog.c
    $ LINK myprog, VMSSQLITE$ROOT:[LIB]LIBSQLITE3.OLB/LIBRARY

With VSI C, compile with 64-bit pointers and case-sensitive names:

    $ CC /POINTER_SIZE=64 /NAMES=AS_IS /INCLUDE=VMSSQLITE$ROOT:[INCLUDE] myprog.c

and link either statically, as above, or against the shareable image, so
that a later kit updates SQLite without relinking:

    $ LINK myprog, SYS$INPUT/OPTIONS
    SQLITE3$SHR/SHAREABLE

A program linked so finds the image through the logical name SQLITE3$SHR at
run time.  The image's symbol vector only grows (GSMATCH LEQUAL 3,54): a
program linked against this kit runs with any later kit of SQLite 3.

SQLite on OpenVMS: what differs
-------------------------------
- The file layer.  The C RTL's file I/O is not coherent between processes,
  so this SQLite does its I/O with $QIO, keeps the end of file in the file
  header, and takes its locks on a companion file.  Several processes and
  accounts can share a database; transactions are atomic across a process
  stopped at any moment (STOP/ID).
- The lock file.  Each database has a <database>-lock beside it (TEST.DB
  has TEST.DB-LOCK), which SQLite never deletes.  To delete or rename a
  database, do the same to its lock file (and to any -JOURNAL), with no
  process using it.
- Access.  Every account that writes a database needs write access to the
  database, its lock file and its directory (the journal is created
  there).  Use UIC protection or ACLs on all three.  The first process to
  open a database creates its lock file, with that process's default
  protection.
- Rollback journals only.  WAL needs memory shared between processes,
  which the C RTL maps per UIC group: PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL leaves the
  journal mode as it was.  Memory-mapped I/O (mmap_size) is off.
- No loadable extensions (load_extension, .load); FTS5, R*Tree, JSON and
  the math functions are built in.
- Temporary files go to SYS$SCRATCH.
- Each transaction costs more than on Unix (locks, $QIO and file header
  calls; about 1.3 ms for an autocommit SELECT on the test system): put
  many statements in one transaction.

Diagnostics
-----------
    $ DEFINE VMSSQLITE_ERRORS DISK$X:[DIR]

makes each process write the file layer's errors, with their VMS status,
to VMSSQLITE_ERR_<pid>.LOG in that directory (made at the first error).

    $ DEFINE VMSSQLITE_TRACE DISK$X:[DIR]

makes each process log every lock, sync, open and I/O count to
VMSSQLITE_<pid>.LOG there; it slows the process down.
